Z||000007||deceler8||11-03-2000||11:08 PM||||Yeah...love that sig. But I'm always forgetting to use it. <P>I think one of the reasons we end up with the candidates we do is that politics is so dirty...it's become fair game to dredge up all the slime.<P>Who in their right mind would subject themselves to that...you got it...the folks with the deep pockets and the short memories.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tigger||11-06-2000||10:10 AM||||Yea the DUI thing comes as a low blow, but I don't think Al Gore or his campain is to blame for bringing it up.  Looked like some democratic crack pot found out about it and wanted to become famous by bringing it forward.<P>Bush's immediate response didn't go well with me.  Making the excuse that he didn't want to bring it up for his daughters sake was lame.  Don't you think if you made mistakes at a young age you would want to teach your own kids about not making those same mistakes?  Go figure.<P>It wasn't till the next day in Saginaw Michigan that he finally said he had "learned" from his mistake.  That's something he should have said the night the news was brought up, hell he had 25 years to think about what he would say if it ever came up.  <P>Well, it was another missed opportunity for my vote.||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||11-06-2000||11:02 AM||||I agree that it was very short sighted of Mr. Bush to imagine that this incident would never come up during the campaign. It would have been better if Mr. Bush would have disclosed it during the primaries, for example. <P>I suspect this isn't gonna hurt him, as both Clinton and Gore have bigger skeletons in their closets.<P>But one thing you have to admit is that the Governor's father didn't try to cover this up or make it go away...George W. paid his fine and had his license suspended...<P>As I recall, Mr. Bush also tested just barely above the legal limit...there but for the grace of God go I...<P>And driving under the influence just wasn't that big a deal back then...if George Sr. really wanted to make it go away, he certainly could have.<P>And there will be no outcry from MADD or any of these other groups, mainly because many of their constituents have been down that road.<P>I go both ways on the daughter issue...kids don't need to know how their parents have failed...just weakens them in their kids eyes. But the daughters know now...and what a lousy way to find out.||||reg||1
Z||000010||frontal lobe||11-06-2000||02:58 PM||||Tigger, not picking on you but you said he didn't say he learned from his mistake until the next day.  Did he have to say it??  He quit drinking alcohol 16 YEARS AGO.  16 YEARS AGO.  If he quit drinking 16 years ago, does he have to SAY he learned from his mistake?  Isn't it obvious?<P>Regarding not bringing it up to his children:  you can bet there are a BUNCH of stupid things I did growing up that I would not tell my 3 daughters.  If it comes up, fine, I'll discuss it with them.  And they already know I'm not perfect.  But for me to sit them down and recite all my mistakes is unnecessary.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tigger||11-06-2000||09:27 PM||||I wouldn't care whether he had is last drink, 16 years ago, 14 years ogo, who cares, that's not the point...  <P>Bush should have taken responsibility for his own actions, by saying he had learned from them.  Instead he continuously blamed Al Gore for bringing it up which we all found out to be not true.<P>It's called taking RESPONSIBILITY for your own actions...  putting blame on someone else is NOT what I call taking RESPONSIBILITY.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-06-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000012||frontal lobe||11-07-2000||12:01 AM||||Tigger, you either don't get it because you aren't thinking or you don't WANT to get it.<P>He paid his time and served whatever he was supposed to back then.  He didn't try to have his dad get him out of it.  He got control of his life and I REPEAT BECAUSE IT SEEMS YOU DON'T GET IT:  QUIT DRINKING 16 YEARS AGO.  ANY person with a few functioning brain cells would interpret these CONSISTENT actions as taking responsibility.<P>I think you would rather have him "take responsibility" the democrat way:  SAY you have learned from it and that you are sorry, then DON'T change and just keep on doing it again but just be more careful not to get caught again.<P>I don't blame him for challenging Gore about THE TIMING of the "announcement".  The slime-ball who brought it up could have revealed it ANY time.  You explain to me the timing of it.<P>Now, since you have established the standard of behavior, I am waiting for your public declaration on the forum of the mistakes you have made and whether or not you have learned from them.  Well...||||reg||1
Z||000013||Shiner||11-07-2000||12:48 AM||||Speaking of the candidates children..<BR>Isn't Al Gore's daughter pretty damn cute?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Tigger||11-07-2000||02:27 AM||||Well at least we both agree here, that's probably the best thing his Dad ever did for him.  By the way, how do you really know for sure he quit drinking 16 years ago?  You must personally know Gov. Bush a whole lot better than any of us do to make a statement like that.<P>I've already explained my thoughts on the timing of the "announcement", read my first post in this thread.  Yes, it's there, go ahead and scroll up if you don't believe me.  Did I mention it was a crack pot democrat that brought it up anyway for his own interests?  I bet Bush is probably happy we're all talking about this and not his sketchy illegal drug use that Al Gore has never mentioned.  I better call Al right now and remind him of that one, maybe it's not too late.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Well, I have never drove a car intoxicated if that's what your asking.  Unfortunately, I had to learn the hard way by someone elses mistake.  I'd like to think I learned a lot that day, watching my Grandfather being airlifted from the field next to the accident and watching a guy stammering around his car trying to figure out where he was at.  Only thing I can ever hope for is the guy learned from his mistake, and if for some reason if he decides to run for President, then I would hope he would say "Yes, It was a long time ago, I didn't want my daughters to know, I paid the penalty, and Yes, I learned from my mistake." and not "Yes, It was a long time ago, I didn't want my daughters to know... and boy isn't it just funny you bring this up now?"<P>I'm not establishing any standards of behavior here.  I just think it was a missed opportunity for him to genuinely say that he had learned from his mistake that night.||||reg||1
Z||000015||frontal lobe||11-07-2000||09:30 AM||||Tigger, I was making an assumption.  I had assumed you would have seen on the news that he DID say he learned from his mistake.  He was in Milwaukee when the announcement was made.  All the local news stations broadcast him making his statement and SAYING he learned from it.  My assumption that the national media would air that was stupid.  I forgot about their consistent policy of censorship by deciding what they want to report.<P>Having said that, I still believe his consistent behavior in not drinking alcohol for 16 years DEMONSTRATES his having learned, vs. just SAYING it and have no intention of changing, like many do.  Talk is cheap.<P>As for me not knowing Bush well enough to HAVE PROOF that he has not had alcohol, that is true.  However, I am quite confident he hasn't know that our fine national investigative reporters of the mainstream media would have gleefully reported any instances where he had.||||reg||1
